## Methodology and limitations

- This is a screening aid. It is not a legal determination, a consumer report under the FCRA, or a GDPR assessment, and it should not be the sole basis for employment, credit, housing or similar decisions.
- Sources are public web pages found through search providers. Pages that disallow automated access (robots.txt, authorization required) or sit behind paywalls are not retrieved; absence of findings is not evidence of absence.
- VERIFIED: supported by at least {{ r.meta.min_independent_domains }} independent domains or by a primary or top-tier source. SINGLE_SOURCE: one source only, uncorroborated. CONFLICTED: sources disagree on the outcome; shown as disputed, never as established. These states are not probabilities.
- Legal-status labels (alleged, charged, convicted, settled, dismissed/acquitted) are inferred from text by fixed rules and can be wrong. Confirm against court or agency records.
- Identity: people and organizations can share names. Matching uses the name and the context given; confirm role, location and employer before relying on a finding.
- Dates come from source metadata, then passage text, then the URL. Findings outside the lookback window are excluded; undated findings are flagged.
- Only the executive summary is model-written. It is limited to cited claims and checked by fixed rules. All other text is generated from the data in report.json.
